<Item XName="Font" Type="Font">
<Angle><T>Int</T><D>0</D></Angle>
<Color><T>Int</T><D>0</D></Color>
<Italic><T>Int</T><D>0</D></Italic>
<Name>Arial</Name>
<Size><T>Double</T><D>500</D></Size>
<Weight><T>Int</T><D>400</D></Weight></Item>
<Item XName="Text" Type="Param">
<DataEntry>&apos;&#x000D;&#x000A;The bitstream of L1CP signal is modulated on L1 carrier frequency using TMBOC modulation technique. &#x000D;&#x000A;The L1CP TMBOC technique uses a mixture of BOC (1, 1) spreading symbols and BOC(6,1) spreading &#x000D;&#x000A;symbols, where each BOC(6,1) spreading symbol consists of 6 cycles of a 6 x 1.023 MHz squarewave, &#x000D;&#x000A;defined as binary 101010101010 (1= binary bit value), with total duration 1/1.023 microseconds &#x000D;&#x000A;(see Figure 3.3-2b).&#x000D;&#x000A;&#x000D;&#x000A;The pattern of BOC(1,1) and BOC(6,1) spreading symbols repeats every 10230 spreading symbols &#x000D;&#x000A;corresponding to a new bit of L1CO-code. Let the index of the spreading symbols L1CP be t = 0, 1, &#x000D;&#x000A;. . ., 10229, where t = 0 is the first spreading symbol in the next bit of L1CO-code. Write t as &#x000D;&#x000A;t = ut + 33 vt, where ut = 0, . . ., 32 and vt = 0, . . ., 309. Then all spreading symbols in L1CP are&#x000D;&#x000A;BOC(1,1), except for those that are BOC(6,1) that occur for those t with ut =0, 4, 6, and 29 (i.e,&#x000D;&#x000A;t = 0, 4, 6, 29, 33, 37, 39, 62, &#x2026;, 10197, 10201, 10203, 10226). This pattern is shown in &#x000D;&#x000A;Figure 3.3-2c. The BOC(1,1) and BOC(6,1) spreading symbols are defined using sine-phasing, &#x000D;&#x000A;so are aligned with bits of L1CPcode.&#x000D;&#x000A;</DataEntry></Item></Item>
